{
  "case_id": "2010-10-10",
  "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:02 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Seth P. Waxman",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Due process requires the assistance of counsel before an alleged civil contemner can be incarcerated."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We'll hear argument first this morning in case 10-10, Turner v. Rogers."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Seth P. Waxman",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0000-0002",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "According to the government statistics, 70 percent of noncustodial parents either have no income or have income less than $10,000, and, therefore, in a State -- in every State that accepts TANF funds, which is every State, they are represented by the State agency, and South Carolina in this case has made a rule that in-State cases -- and that also includes nonwelfare cases where the -- a custodial parent has chosen to be represented by the State -- the State entirely carries its prosecutorial burden by filing a rule to show cause and an affidavit showing the arrearages, and that places the burden, which South Carolina says is a heavy burden, on the defendant to prove inability to comply as a condition of maintaining his liberty."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "My understanding is that it's a commonplace if the witness declines to testify even though the witness has immunity, or the attorney or the witness declines to produce a document, the judge says you will remain in jail until you comply."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Why is it that -\u00ad"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Kruger",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0001-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. KRUGER: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: In civil contempt proceedings to enforce orders for child support, due process requires a meaningful opportunity to be heard on the simple and straightforward, but critical, question that characterizes remedial sanctions in this area: whether the nonpaying parent has the ability to pay."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Waxman",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 0.6,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0001-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "May I reserve the balance of my time?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Counsel, did -- just to be clear, your answer to the question presented is no, right? It was not error for the South Carolina court to say there's no constitutional right in this type of a proceeding to appointed counsel?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But just -- just assume that we could somehow block out that category. If you could focus just on the domestic relations support proceedings, would there be a basic change in the way those proceedings are being conducted in other States, if we ruled in favor of Petitioner and said there's an absolute right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Bibas",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Yes, Justice Kennedy, there'd be a massive change. Trial judges need to know ahead of time which sets of procedures to apply, civil or criminal."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Isn't it -- isn't it true that most States in child support cases, when the defendant says I have no money, will appoint counsel?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Bibas",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That is not true of most States, Justice Ginsburg. My understanding is 15 States recognize it as a constitutional matter, 11 additional States by statute, rule, or practice appear to recognize a statewide right to counsel. So, there's a bear majority. At least 17 States do not have a statewide right, and the remaining 7 are unclear."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "All right? What's wrong with that rule?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Government is not a party here, is it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0002-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Counsel, do you know why we're not hearing from the State of South Carolina?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0002-0008",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Because the State was not a party to the proceeding."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0002-0009",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In New Jersey, after the State supreme court recognized in Pasqua a right to appointed counsel, New Jersey stopped using civil contempt enforcement."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0002-0010",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There are effectively no rules of hearsay or authentication."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE KENNEDY",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It's a little difficult to write the opinion, if you are to prevail, saying there's no absolute right, but there might be in some other case, depending."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE SCALIA",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Counsel, I don't understand how we could say that if you do not meet minimum due process procedures, you must meet more than minimum due process procedures."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. BIBAS",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0003-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I -- I don't believe that it's necessary because we can't conceive of a legal issue here so complex that categorically a lawyer is necessary."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE KAGAN",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0003-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But, Mr. Bibas, practically when those forms are used, the person fills out a form, and mostly they're just accepted, aren't they?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. BIBAS",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0003-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the closest analogue in the legal system to the question here about inability to pay child support is inability to afford counsel. Our criminal justice legal system has extensive experience with that under the CJA, the Criminal Justice Act."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. WAXMAN",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0003-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Our suggestion is he could be incarcerated so that he could be placed on work release in a county jail program and reduce his arrearage."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE ALITO",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0003-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 62,
        "start": 62
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, we don't have the Social Security disability form, but if the judge credited that and accepted that your client is -- was unable to work and had only the assets listed on that form, would he not then automatically be -- have shown that he had an inability to pay?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. WAXMAN",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0003-0009",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 62,
        "start": 62
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think the answer is yes, but we don't know whether the judge even looked at the form, and we know from South Carolina law that a mere assertion is not the marshaling of evidentiary support that's required to carry the burden."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ca2fa0b8bae823d3aeaf15c67d7d10d1b93024194055e8f5019654faea95b78a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ca2fa0b8bae8-0003-0010",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 62,
        "start": 62
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2010-10-10",
      "confidence": 0.0,
